00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:25,000
The very first tip was really small, you know we we wanted to go try this and it was University Drive West West of Research Park Boulevard and it helped bring West Side Center with a target center is we made a small little improvement for the state of Alabama for

350
00:53:25,000 --> 00:53:42,000
$172 an intersection, and that thing was paid off a blink of an eye was almost a test for us so that did not have an education piece. The only other one that I can recall that did not actually did was TIFF six and that was the players, and we contemplated

351
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:56,000
building a new school out there as things progressed we realized working with the school system, the location of where we proposed that wasn't the right spot long term. Even though it was in the plan we did not build it. But those are the only two out of nine.

352
00:53:56,000 --> 00:54:13,000
Councilman Meredith knows we're working on possibly another TIFF district in limestone County would have a significant value to our school system out there to help them with with growth and infrastructure so we try to look and make sure we realize that

353
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:30,000
that new deferral of new taxes not deferral of any existing tax base but that new deferral is try to go help our tax recipients with something that they have upcoming or need, then you get in this district so everyone tries to get some wind out of the district,

354
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:33,000
while it's active.

355
00:54:33,000 --> 00:54:50,000
I was just clarifying that that was actually going to be one of my first questions because it always comes up with the tips, is it going to negatively impact revenue to the school system in the short term. And the answer is no, that they will continue to receive the same tax revenues that they are receiving now.

356
00:54:50,000 --> 00:55:15,000
And in the long term, as we have seen pay off recently with the most Toyota tip, they will see an increase in revenue so it will benefit the school system. You noted on your timeline when the Madison County Commission would approve the TIFF plan, is the Board of Education required to also approve the TIFF plan?

357
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:27,000
So by state law there are only two governing bodies that have to adopt any TIFF district, one that is the county governing body whether it's in Madison County or Limestone County and this body.

358
00:55:27,000 --> 00:55:41,000
But they have been informed. Yeah, we were required to inform all tax recipients, but the only two tax recipients that have to vote on the district is a Madison County Commission in this case, and the Huntsville City Council.

359
00:55:41,000 --> 00:55:56,000
We're going to have a joint meeting with the school system on June 1st and if it is appropriate at that time perhaps if there are any questions from any board members about TIFF 9, that might be an appropriate time to maybe have a discussion about it.

360
00:55:56,000 --> 00:56:07,000
Absolutely. And we'll also be down the road a little bit on our plan, on formalizing our plan as well. A question has been asked regarding the VBC expansion, that's a really good question.

361
00:56:07,000 --> 00:56:16,000
Why is the VBC expansion so important and why are we investing so much money in that? What is the community benefit?

362
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:22,000
If I can zoom out a little more than the VBC and then I'll answer specifically to the Vaughan-Bron Center expansion.

363
00:56:22,000 --> 00:56:37,000
So within the TIFF statute of Alabama law, one thing we have to do is define a district and within that district there are certain qualifying parameters to qualify for a TIFF district.

364
00:56:37,000 --> 00:56:54,000
So there's always a balance between how large to make the district to do as many projects as you want to do, but also keep in mind the organic growth that just is generally happening in our community due to new jobs and population growth.

365
00:56:54,000 --> 00:57:12,000
We do not want to over capture that new growth and further defer new tax base to our tax recipients. So there's this balance of how big do you make it or how small do you make it such that you get what you think is the top priorities that are occurring within that area of the district,

366
00:57:12,000 --> 00:57:26,000
whether that's in Limestone County, the need for utilities and infrastructure due to the growth or new education facilities, or in this case downtown to say look, there's opportunities to continue to push downtown forward,

367
00:57:26,000 --> 00:57:38,000
continue to implement the downtown master plan that's been adopted by the Planning Commission in this body. So there's not an exact answer to do that. It's a science.

368
00:57:38,000 --> 00:57:52,000
We do a lot of modeling. Okay, we go capture what you see on this screen. What is the possibility you see the circles there that kind of show where we see pockets of area of potential new growth.

369
00:57:52,000 --> 00:58:07,000
But we didn't want to go really far south or let me give you an example, go all the way to the new northern bypass. We could do that and pick up the new North Village Town Center, which is 660,000 square feet of new retail that

370
00:58:07,000 --> 00:58:23,000
you know, have a tax basis of about $300 a square foot annually, which not on the books. So it's not paying tax, we could win grab that. We don't think that's fair, and we could have just continue this North Beltline Greenway all the way up to that area.

371
00:58:23,000 --> 00:58:40,000
We don't think that's fair to our education system nor Madison County. So we try to keep it concise in doing so. So we look at active projects that have been on the books, or ones that we know will move the needle from a tax base.

372
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:56,000
And we go say okay, what are those projects on the books that we can't ordinarily afford to do without new tax base, the bomb bronze center has been on the list for a very long time.

373
00:58:56,000 --> 00:59:17,000
Our conferences, some of our biggest ones at USA and others are to the point that the community has grown, those conferences have grown, such that everybody that wants to have a booth or attend those conferences, and they have millions of dollars of economic impact annually to our general fund,

374
00:59:17,000 --> 00:59:33,000
can't get to the conference. I think this past one in the spring they had 60 something vendors, which are defense contractors in different organizations that said we want to attend, we want to pay for a booth, and they said we're full.

375
00:59:33,000 --> 00:59:48,000
Well now you're starting to have conversations, we love Huntsville, we've been coming here a long time, but we need to find a new venue. We need to find a venue to everyone who wants to participate in this national convention can actually participate.

376
00:59:48,000 --> 01:00:06,000
And you look around us you have Birmingham who has made upgrades to the BJCC, Mobile is building a brand new civic center, Chattanooga has made expansions, Nashville, one of our big competitors, Savannah has just completed $600 million, Knoxville is looking.

377
01:00:06,000 --> 01:00:19,000
So in and around us there are options to take the conferences that we have to other communities, which would be a significant hit to our general fund. I'm not saying that's the reason to go expand the VBC, I think we're at this inflection point.

378
01:00:19,000 --> 01:00:28,000
You know it's our inflection point to have a civic center that holds graduations, occasional music and plays, and it really not be a convention center.

379
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:37,000
And you start looking at the investments with hotels and actually what we can continue to accelerate, we're at an inflection point, what do we want the Bonron Center to be?

380
01:00:37,000 --> 01:00:48,000
So we started looking at this, it was a hard one to budget, it is a big number, but we said look we don't want it to be, we don't want to come to you with one project, that didn't make sense.

381
01:00:48,000 --> 01:00:58,000
So really what we started doing is working the VBC and say okay, how tight can you get this estimate? Because these other four projects we're going to go do.

382
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:04,000
We would love to put the VBC in with those four projects and that's what we brought you.

383
01:01:04,000 --> 01:01:18,000
Thank you. And now if you could answer the other questions that have come from the public, most of which were about the priorities, you've addressed the VBC, but other priorities that we have here and why this project is important for the community.

