02:31:19,040 --> 02:31:23,440
of the contract, we are increasing their gross revenue share to HVGA.

1046
02:31:23,440 --> 02:31:26,440
So when we think about it in the existing agreement, there's really two ways in which

1047
02:31:26,440 --> 02:31:30,560
HVGA can earn money.

1048
02:31:30,560 --> 02:31:34,800
One is a share of gross revenues as just a base management fee.

1049
02:31:34,800 --> 02:31:38,400
And then there is a structured net profit share.

1050
02:31:38,400 --> 02:31:43,340
One of the challenges and I'll have some supplements to get a little bit more detail.

1051
02:31:43,340 --> 02:31:49,080
In the existing agreement, the way the net profit was structured, we now know in hindsight

1052
02:31:49,080 --> 02:31:51,520
it was essentially impossible for them to earn it.

1053
02:31:51,520 --> 02:31:58,240
And so the reason you want to line up an incentive around profitability is to incentivize behavior.

1054
02:31:58,240 --> 02:32:01,280
Incentivize maximizing revenues and minimizing expenses.

1055
02:32:01,280 --> 02:32:04,920
But if you make that incentive impossible to achieve, it doesn't necessarily affect

1056
02:32:04,920 --> 02:32:06,040
the behavior you're looking for.

1057
02:32:06,040 --> 02:32:09,320
So we're looking to restructure that in a way where they really do have an opportunity

1058
02:32:09,320 --> 02:32:10,320
to earn it.

1059
02:32:10,320 --> 02:32:13,520
If they really earn it is because they're doing extremely well and we're making more

1060
02:32:13,520 --> 02:32:14,520
money.

1061
02:32:14,520 --> 02:32:17,280
But ultimately you need to restructure that to make that actually function as an incentive

1062
02:32:17,280 --> 02:32:19,080
to them.

1063
02:32:19,080 --> 02:32:23,080
We do significantly increase our monthly and quarterly financial reporting requirements

1064
02:32:23,080 --> 02:32:24,840
off of the existing agreement.

1065
02:32:24,840 --> 02:32:30,320
And we also in the existing agreement, the capex that we set as the price that a promoter

1066
02:32:30,320 --> 02:32:35,400
has to contribute towards future to that, to the capex and to the maintenance account.

1067
02:32:35,400 --> 02:32:37,160
It had no escalators out in the future.

1068
02:32:37,160 --> 02:32:40,240
It did not contemplate inflation.

1069
02:32:40,240 --> 02:32:43,640
In conversation with them, we felt like there ought to be some step up in the future for

1070
02:32:43,640 --> 02:32:46,120
promoters to pay in order to contribute towards that.

1071
02:32:46,120 --> 02:32:48,440
So this does provide that in future years.

1072
02:32:48,440 --> 02:32:51,000
I think the first step up isn't until 2030.

1073
02:32:51,000 --> 02:32:52,000
There's a couple of step ups.

1074
02:32:52,000 --> 02:33:00,920
So they're in the out years to improve that contribution into the capital accounts.

1075
02:33:00,920 --> 02:33:05,460
So if you use that, I'm going to use FY25 actual numbers here to kind of estimate for

1076
02:33:05,460 --> 02:33:10,280
you how this money moves.

1077
02:33:10,280 --> 02:33:14,360
Because FY25, we think was a good year at the Orion.

1078
02:33:14,360 --> 02:33:17,720
We feel like as we look at the first four years and kind of what we see, we think it

1079
02:33:17,720 --> 02:33:22,260
is fairly representative of what we ought to see going forward.

1080
02:33:22,260 --> 02:33:25,460
So on the left is the way it's currently structured.

1081
02:33:25,460 --> 02:33:33,440
Under a 5% gross revenue share for their management fee, HVGA got just short of $200,000.

1082
02:33:33,440 --> 02:33:38,320
But the profit incentive was set up on net of the entire operation at the percentage

1083
02:33:38,320 --> 02:33:39,680
of breakdown you see there.

1084
02:33:39,680 --> 02:33:44,160
Well, the way that is structured and with food and beverage being outside of the revenue

1085
02:33:44,160 --> 02:33:48,960
stream for the Orion, there realistically was no way for them to ever earn that.

1086
02:33:48,960 --> 02:33:52,600
So they got zero off that profit incentive.

1087
02:33:52,600 --> 02:34:00,400
But HVGA FMB, the separate company, we did get as required by the lease payment, 7% of

1088
02:34:00,400 --> 02:34:04,120
the gross revenues, but ultimately the net income, so the profitability of the food and

1089
02:34:04,120 --> 02:34:07,520
beverage business was about $1.7 million.

1090
02:34:07,520 --> 02:34:10,200
Now that shouldn't be surprising.

1091
02:34:10,200 --> 02:34:13,360
When you look at any venue like this, the most profitable segment of the business is

1092
02:34:13,360 --> 02:34:17,360
going to be food and beverage with a lot of emphasis on beverage.

1093
02:34:17,360 --> 02:34:19,920
And you can kind of see that by the nature of events.

1094
02:34:19,920 --> 02:34:23,080
There's some events where there's going to be more alcohol consumption, there's going

1095
02:34:23,080 --> 02:34:26,560
to be some where there's very little and you can see the spin per head float up and down

1096
02:34:26,560 --> 02:34:27,560
across those concerts.

1097
02:34:27,560 --> 02:34:34,000
You can really see how food and beverage drives the revenue potential of a venue like this.

1098
02:34:34,000 --> 02:34:39,440
That's true at the VBC and other places where we have these kind of operations.

1099
02:34:39,440 --> 02:34:46,440
So in the aggregate, TBG, the corporate, between its two subsidiaries here functioning in and

1100
02:34:46,440 --> 02:34:54,880
around the Orion took out about a $1.9 million net income that they earned off the Orion.

1101
02:34:54,880 --> 02:35:02,480
Now when you look at our budgeting and you just look at this year's budget, FY26, we

1102
02:35:02,480 --> 02:35:08,400
have had to budget in the general fund about $2 million, using round figures, in order

1103
02:35:08,400 --> 02:35:11,440
to balance the budget at the Orion.

1104
02:35:11,440 --> 02:35:16,840
And that lines up very, very closely with what we're seeing go out and go to their corporate

1105
02:35:16,840 --> 02:35:19,040
headquarters off the food and beverage business.

1106
02:35:19,040 --> 02:35:25,120
And so this is really about pulling that back into the Orion.

1107
02:35:25,120 --> 02:35:29,240
And so what you see on the right is the way those same set of numbers, so using trying

1108
02:35:29,240 --> 02:35:32,600
to do an apples to apples comparison, again using FY25 actuals.

1109
02:35:32,600 --> 02:35:37,840
So had this new structure been in place for FY25, these are the estimates of what would

1110
02:35:37,840 --> 02:35:38,980
have happened there.

1111
02:35:38,980 --> 02:35:41,520
One is you see the increased gross revenue.

1112
02:35:41,520 --> 02:35:45,220
So they would have, their base management fee would have been about $700,000 and they

1113
02:35:45,220 --> 02:35:49,280
actually would have been able to earn some of that profit incentive and that would have

1114
02:35:49,280 --> 02:35:52,200
been about $300,000.

1115
02:35:52,200 --> 02:35:59,040
But the other piece of this new structure is they don't get that $1.7 million.

1116
02:35:59,040 --> 02:36:04,000
But I want to go back up to the top because when we see, hey, they're getting, they're

1117
02:36:04,000 --> 02:36:10,560
able to grow their share, 100% of their compensation is tied to performance.

1118
02:36:10,560 --> 02:36:14,360
If revenues go up, their share goes up, but so does ours.

1119
02:36:14,360 --> 02:36:21,200
So every dollar that revenue goes up, they get $0.08, we get $0.92.

1120
02:36:21,200 --> 02:36:26,320
And so we are just at the top line and up to the top line is not all the way it's at.

1121
02:36:26,320 --> 02:36:29,560
That's just the beginning of a profit loss statement.

1122
02:36:29,560 --> 02:36:33,760
But right off the bat in their base management agreement, we are incentivizing them to maximize

1123
02:36:33,760 --> 02:36:37,560
revenues which can then flow down through the profit loss statement.

1124
02:36:37,560 --> 02:36:42,880
But we also want to make sure that they are focused on the actual net profitability in

1125
02:36:42,880 --> 02:36:46,880
any segment of the business that is actually expected to be profitable and that's in those

1126
02:36:46,880 --> 02:36:47,880
big concerts.

1127
02:36:47,880 --> 02:36:52,320
And so what you'll see in future profit loss statements, one is breaking out so we see

1128
02:36:52,320 --> 02:36:57,280
clear overhead of expenses so we understand the expenses that it takes regardless of what's

1129
02:36:57,280 --> 02:37:02,800
going on at a day at the Orion, but then see the actual profit loss in the net off of the

1130
02:37:02,800 --> 02:37:08,000
concert segment as compared to all those free events that we're doing that are the community

1131
02:37:08,000 --> 02:37:09,000
events.

1132
02:37:09,000 --> 02:37:13,600
And what this allows us to do is when you earn that profit off of the concert business,

1133
02:37:13,600 --> 02:37:17,040
that money can then be used to fund those free events.

1134
02:37:17,040 --> 02:37:24,040
And we believe strongly in continuing to maintain and hopefully grow more and more of that broad,

1135
02:37:24,040 --> 02:37:30,180
very inexpensive or even free where appropriate access to events in the Orion.

1136
02:37:30,180 --> 02:37:32,440
So that's what really this is about.

1137
02:37:32,440 --> 02:37:36,440
So when you compare in the aggregate, so some folks say, okay, how much is TVG up in New

1138
02:37:36,440 --> 02:37:43,320
York City making off of this under today's structure in FY 25 about 1.9 million under

1139
02:37:43,320 --> 02:37:46,600
the new structure, it would have been about 1 million.

1140
02:37:46,600 --> 02:37:50,720
And the rest of that money is staying right here in Huntsville and structured in a way

1141
02:37:50,720 --> 02:37:56,560
that we believe better incentivizes the very financial and budget discipline that we all

1142
02:37:56,560 --> 02:38:04,800
need to have, but particularly in a place like this to drive that profitability.

1143
02:38:04,800 --> 02:38:10,240
So I've had some folks, let me just hit y'all with some questions I've received.

1144
02:38:10,240 --> 02:38:12,240
And then I certainly want to hear your questions.

1145
02:38:12,240 --> 02:38:16,240
I've had folks say, okay, we're investing $12 million.

1146
02:38:16,240 --> 02:38:17,240
What's the payback on that?

1147
02:38:17,240 --> 02:38:18,240
How do we get our money back?

1148
02:38:18,240 --> 02:38:22,080
And I would say, first off, we need to think and understand where that money goes and for

1149
02:38:22,080 --> 02:38:23,660
what purpose.

1150
02:38:23,660 --> 02:38:28,400
The first portion of that, that $8 million, you're buying a real asset.

1151
02:38:28,400 --> 02:38:33,280
And at the end of five years, 10 years, 15 years, 20 years, you still own those buildings,

1152
02:38:33,280 --> 02:38:37,720
you still own those improvements like you do other city facilities.

1153
02:38:37,720 --> 02:38:40,500
And so that asset will continue to retain value.

1154
02:38:40,500 --> 02:38:43,800
And so trying to compare, okay, how much of that $8 million do I still have?

1155
02:38:43,800 --> 02:38:48,680
Obviously, we could get appraisals every so often and put a number to it, but it's not

1156
02:38:48,680 --> 02:38:53,620
dissimilar to other capital investments we make in terms of building buildings and making

1157
02:38:53,620 --> 02:38:54,620
improvements and stuff.

1158
02:38:54,620 --> 02:38:56,800
We own those assets where right now today we don't.

1159
02:38:56,800 --> 02:38:58,440
So we're buying real assets.

1160
02:38:58,440 --> 02:39:00,920
I had somebody say, hey, you're just giving them a gift.

1161
02:39:00,920 --> 02:39:03,200
No, we're, you know, starting with the $8 million piece.

1162
02:39:03,200 --> 02:39:07,960
We are buying real things that have been inventoried and appraised and we have a real appraised

1163
02:39:07,960 --> 02:39:09,600
value on them today.

1164
02:39:09,600 --> 02:39:12,920
And the price we're getting is a little bit below that appraised value.

1165
02:39:12,920 --> 02:39:16,680
Then the other piece is the operations.

1166
02:39:16,680 --> 02:39:21,980
And we are essentially buying an operating successful business.

1167
02:39:21,980 --> 02:39:27,840
Now if one of us wanted to go buy, you know, a high performing restaurant downtown and

1168
02:39:27,840 --> 02:39:31,480
you're just taking over the business as it exists and you're getting all of the menus

1169
02:39:31,480 --> 02:39:35,720
and all of the staff and all the equipment and the building and everything, certainly

1170
02:39:35,720 --> 02:39:39,600
there'd be a value to the building and the tables and the chairs and things, but there

1171
02:39:39,600 --> 02:39:43,560
would also be value to those future potential revenue streams.

1172
02:39:43,560 --> 02:39:47,000
But in that example, you've actually got to go do work and earn those things.

1173
02:39:47,000 --> 02:39:48,820
And there's obviously work to be done here.

1174
02:39:48,820 --> 02:39:54,760
But what HPG Food and Beverage has is a contractual right to those revenues.

1175
02:39:54,760 --> 02:39:58,400
And so if somebody asks, well, why don't you just cancel the contract and kick them out?

1176
02:39:58,400 --> 02:39:59,400
Well, they have a legal right.

1177
02:39:59,400 --> 02:40:05,120
You know, we and the appropriate authorities approved and signed that contract.

1178
02:40:05,120 --> 02:40:08,840
And so, you know, if we just said, hey, get out of here, we're taking over, they'd say,

1179
02:40:08,840 --> 02:40:09,840
well, we'll see you in court.

1180
02:40:09,840 --> 02:40:12,160
And they would win that case because they have real contractual rights.

1181
02:40:12,160 --> 02:40:17,300
And we need to honor those legal obligations that we collectively entered into.

1182
02:40:17,300 --> 02:40:23,880
So the net present value of those reasonably modeled future revenues is about $10 million.

1183
02:40:23,880 --> 02:40:26,960
And we have been in negotiations for a while.

1184
02:40:26,960 --> 02:40:30,920
As you can imagine, they started at a bigger number than what we ultimately agreed to.

1185
02:40:30,920 --> 02:40:38,200
So what we have agreed to is the $4 million upfront plus for HVGA some improved revenue

1186
02:40:38,200 --> 02:40:41,520
streams, but HVGF and B, it goes to zero.

1187
02:40:41,520 --> 02:40:43,880
And so you see how those numbers behave.

1188
02:40:43,880 --> 02:40:49,060
But when you look at that, you know, that contract component of it, you know, for improving

1189
02:40:49,060 --> 02:40:55,440
our overall profitability there by that $1.5 to $2 million range, I'm just going to fluctuate

1190
02:40:55,440 --> 02:40:58,760
year to year as the concert business goes.

1191
02:40:58,760 --> 02:41:01,720
And over time with inflation, that number gets bigger.

1192
02:41:01,720 --> 02:41:07,240
But you're really the $4 million, you're getting back in about two and a half to three years.

1193
02:41:07,240 --> 02:41:13,400
And then you continue to improve the impact on the general fund.

1194
02:41:13,400 --> 02:41:14,720
What do I think happens this year?

1195
02:41:14,720 --> 02:41:18,960
Because obviously we're making a decision midseason.

1196
02:41:18,960 --> 02:41:23,640
You know, as I said, in FY26, we need to budget about $2 million in order to balance the budget.

1197
02:41:23,640 --> 02:41:27,840
I think we see that number get substantially better because of this.

1198
02:41:27,840 --> 02:41:31,160
So you know, it's not going to have a full year impact on FY26 because a chunk of the

1199
02:41:31,160 --> 02:41:32,920
season is already gone.

1200
02:41:32,920 --> 02:41:37,560
But we do see immediate impact in this fiscal year to improve that number.

1201
02:41:37,560 --> 02:41:42,160
And then you see the full year impact starting really on one October, so we can get you a

1202
02:41:42,160 --> 02:41:43,160
full year number.

1203
02:41:43,160 --> 02:41:47,160
So, you know, I don't want to say that here halfway through the year, we're going to have

1204
02:41:47,160 --> 02:41:52,160
that full $1.7 to $2 million impact because we're like say we're halfway through the year.

1205
02:41:52,160 --> 02:41:55,600
But we definitely see an appreciable improvement.

1206
02:41:55,600 --> 02:41:59,760
And then you go into the full year impact starting on one October and beyond.

1207
02:41:59,760 --> 02:42:05,920
So that's, you know, those are the assets that were proposed to buy and how we see it

1208
02:42:05,920 --> 02:42:08,040
impacting the financials.

1209
02:42:08,040 --> 02:42:13,080
And I do want to emphasize that budget number is a direct impact to the general fund.

1210
02:42:13,080 --> 02:42:19,360
So at this point, I think open up to questions.

1211
02:42:19,360 --> 02:42:22,720
Mr. members, we have questions.

1212
02:42:22,720 --> 02:42:28,000
Mr. Little and Ms. Watkins.

1213
02:42:28,000 --> 02:42:29,880
Just real quick.

1214
02:42:29,880 --> 02:42:33,760
Original projections to pay down the debt when this was started was about 20 years?

1215
02:42:33,760 --> 02:42:34,760
Yes.

1216
02:42:34,760 --> 02:42:37,400
From day one of Orion's, I went around back then.

1217
02:42:37,400 --> 02:42:39,760
And then there were no new debt for this deal.

1218
02:42:39,760 --> 02:42:40,760
Correct.

1219
02:42:40,760 --> 02:42:42,200
So first off, you know, so what is the debt?

1220
02:42:42,200 --> 02:42:48,040
No, no, this is a project because of the nature of the way we wanted to design and build it

1221
02:42:48,040 --> 02:42:51,920
as well as the way we wanted to manage the debt on it.

1222
02:42:51,920 --> 02:42:56,480
This was turned over to for the public building authority to execute on our behalf.

1223
02:42:56,480 --> 02:42:58,400
People ask me, okay, what other projects that PBA done?

1224
02:42:58,400 --> 02:43:03,000
The PBA built the jail quite a few years ago, and they built this building, they built the

1225
02:43:03,000 --> 02:43:04,200
Orion.

1226
02:43:04,200 --> 02:43:08,240
And there are some other projects, I think, in the out years that we'll evaluate may make

1227
02:43:08,240 --> 02:43:10,440
sense to have the PBA do them in or may not.

1228
02:43:10,440 --> 02:43:14,000
And those are decisions this council would make if we come to those.

1229
02:43:14,000 --> 02:43:18,640
So in this case, the PBA bought the money, the debt load on this was a combination of

1230
02:43:18,640 --> 02:43:19,640
debt and cash.

1231
02:43:19,640 --> 02:43:23,280
But the debt piece was about 37 million.

1232
02:43:23,280 --> 02:43:24,520
And this does not add to that.

1233
02:43:24,520 --> 02:43:27,840
It is a 20 year that's the way we do all of our debt.

1234
02:43:27,840 --> 02:43:30,600
Generally speaking, there's an occasional exception, but generally we do 20 year debt.

1235
02:43:30,600 --> 02:43:31,780
That's what this was.

1236
02:43:31,780 --> 02:43:37,000
So our lease payment, the city's lease payment to the PBA is, you know, each year is the

1237
02:43:37,000 --> 02:43:38,160
amount of their debt service.

1238
02:43:38,160 --> 02:43:39,960
And that's essentially the way the process works out.

1239
02:43:39,960 --> 02:43:40,960
You're right.

1240
02:43:40,960 --> 02:43:46,080
We're not here for that, you know, as part of the council's approval for them to move

1241
02:43:46,080 --> 02:43:51,160
forward, they approve a lease that says we'll pay you whatever your debt service is, they

1242
02:43:51,160 --> 02:43:53,400
now have a guarantee that they can actually pay back the debt.

1243
02:43:53,400 --> 02:43:56,720
And that's what they use to go to the market and borrow the money against that against

1244
02:43:56,720 --> 02:43:58,480
that guaranteed lease payment.

1245
02:43:58,480 --> 02:44:03,600
And so each year we make that debt service payment as a lease payment to the PBA and

1246
02:44:03,600 --> 02:44:04,600
then they pay the debt.

1247
02:44:04,600 --> 02:44:06,320
But it is a 20 year payback.

1248
02:44:06,320 --> 02:44:10,840
We are not proposing to use any debt in this transaction.

1249
02:44:10,840 --> 02:44:17,120
This is this is money available in the capital plan is cash right now.

1250
02:44:17,120 --> 02:44:18,120
Other questions.

1251
02:44:18,120 --> 02:44:19,120
Ms. Watkins.

1252
02:44:19,120 --> 02:44:20,120
Yes.

1253
02:44:20,120 --> 02:44:23,480
Thank you for that presentation, Mr. Hamilton.

1254
02:44:23,480 --> 02:44:26,320
I just have a couple of questions.

1255
02:44:26,320 --> 02:44:35,340
Is the 12 million is going to be payable in one lump sum or over time?

1256
02:44:35,340 --> 02:44:41,680
So it the simple answer to your question is yes, it's paid at closing.

1257
02:44:41,680 --> 02:44:45,240
But if you really dig into the agreement, there's actually some some pieces that we

1258
02:44:45,240 --> 02:44:49,920
hold back in escrow to ensure that there's not any unexpected things as we continue through

1259
02:44:49,920 --> 02:44:51,300
the transition.

1260
02:44:51,300 --> 02:44:58,680
So initially, they would get 11 and a half million, we hold back 500 K. And then we also

1261
02:44:58,680 --> 02:45:03,160
if if if as we went through it, if we found an error in the inventory or found some issue

1262
02:45:03,160 --> 02:45:07,520
that that was their obligation to pay, we could pull it out of that 500 K. And if it

1263
02:45:07,520 --> 02:45:11,320
exceeded that, then we could actually hold back their management fees going forward.

1264
02:45:11,320 --> 02:45:16,760
So we are covered in terms of if there's if there's some issue found post closing.

1265
